// TFR NOTAM text format checked against a live FDC TFR on https://tfr.faa.gov (FDC 6/8784, ZDV). The TFR, METARs, ATIS letters and phone numbers
// in this chapter are fictional. No radio frequencies are given anywhere in this chapter on purpose. Dates assume June and July 2026, MDT = UTC minus 6.
(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).pilot=window.HALCYON_DATA.pilot||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.pilot.full||[]).push({
  num: 4, part: `Part One: The Share`, title: `Airspace`, weeks: `June to July`,
  tasks: [`I.E`,`III.A`,`I.A`,`VI`],
  cast: [
    [`Russ Kim`,`The fourth partner. Travels for work, lives in the club chat, and books Biscuit like it's a conference room.`],
    [`Denver Approach`,`The radar controllers who work the sky around Denver International, including the shelf over Centennial.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `The sectional had been sitting on top of Jordan's refrigerator for three weeks, still in its plastic sleeve, next to a bag of coffee filters and a fire extinguisher she'd bought the same day for the same reason. Things a responsible person owns.`,
    `Tuesday she came off a twenty four hour shift at seven in the morning, slept until one, and ate cereal standing up at the counter. Then she got the sectional down and wrestled it out of the plastic. It unfolded, and unfolded, and kept going, and when it quit it was roughly a twin sheet with print on both sides and Denver hanging off the table into her lap.`,
    `It took her a minute to find home. Denver was a yellow splotch, spilled lemonade. Denver International was out east, a snarl of blue and magenta with runways sticking out of it like straws. And twenty miles south southwest, on the bottom edge of the yellow, a little blue circle with runways in it and CENTENNIAL in small type, sitting in the middle of a nest of blue lines like a bird nobody told about the nest.`,
    `She got a pen and a legal pad and did what she always did with something she didn't understand. She wrote down exactly what she saw, before she let herself decide what any of it meant.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="What the chart shows around Centennial (Jordan's notes, Tuesday)">1. Solid blue lines in rings and wedges centered on Denver International. Inside each piece, a blue fraction. Top number and bottom number. Over the Centennial symbol, the bottom number is 80.<br><br>2. A dashed blue line drawn around Centennial itself. Next to it, a tiny box with a minus sign and 80 in it.<br><br>3. Way out, a thin solid magenta circle around the whole city. Printed on it: MODE C & ADS-B OUT 30 NM. Centennial is well inside it.<br><br>4. Out east on the plains, a fuzzy magenta band, like someone traced a line with a highlighter and smudged it.<br><br>5. Above Denver International, in small type: NO SVFR.<br><br>6. I have no idea what any of this means. Abby says it's a wedding cake.</prop>`,
    `She took a picture of the legal pad and texted it to Abby.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Abby Strand" time="2:41 PM">Good list. Number 1 is the only one that can end my career. Saturday 7am. Briefing room. Bring the chart and the list.</prop>`,
    `Then a minute later, a second bubble: <b>Also there's a chart of Danny's in Biscuit's seat back. Out of date. Don't fly with it. You'll want to see it though.</b>`,
    `Jordan sat there with the chart in her lap for a while. Then she drove to the airport, because it wasn't even three yet, and found it where Abby said. A Denver sectional eight years out of date, from the summer of 2018, folded soft as cloth along the creases, with a coffee ring on Castle Rock and pencil lines out to Akron and Moab and Steamboat. On the Denver panel, the little blue 80 over Centennial had been circled in red pen. Circled three times, hard enough that the pen had gone through the paper on the last lap.`,
    `Next to it, in his block capitals: <b>80!!!</b>`,
    `She didn't know what it meant. She put it in her flight bag anyway.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Abby's Ground School: who owns the sky, and how to ask them`,
    sections: [
      { h: `The alphabet, and who's in charge of it`,
        body: [`Unfold the whole thing. Yes, all of it. Weigh the corners down with the coffee cups, and if you knock mine over, you're buying the next one. Today's the day the colors on that chart stop being decoration. Every line on there is a rule somebody can enforce, and the FAA has a whole office whose job is drawing them.`,
          `Start with the big split. Controlled airspace is Classes A, B, C, D, and E. That's where air traffic control provides service, and depending on the class, where you need to talk to them, be equipped for them, or have their permission. Uncontrolled airspace is Class G. Nobody's controlling traffic in Class G, but the weather minimums still apply there, and so does every other rule in Part 91. Uncontrolled doesn't mean lawless. It means you're the controller.`,
          `Class A runs from 18,000 feet MSL up to and including FL600, and everything in it flies IFR. It isn't even drawn on your sectional, because no VFR pilot is supposed to be there. Biscuit couldn't get there on a good day with a tailwind and a prayer, so we'll leave it there.`,
          `Class E is everything controlled that isn't A, B, C, or D. Where the chart doesn't say otherwise, it starts at 14,500 feet MSL. But almost nowhere is otherwise. Over most of the country it starts 1,200 feet above the ground. Around airports with instrument approaches it comes down to 700 feet, and the chart shows that with a fuzzy magenta band, the vignette, which is item 4 on your list. Where the floor is something other than 700 and it borders Class G, the band is blue. Some airports have Class E right down to the surface, drawn with a dashed magenta line. Above FL600 it's Class E again, which you will never need to know except on a test.`,
          `Class G is whatever's left, from the ground up to wherever the Class E starts over your head. Out on the eastern plains, away from everything, you can be in Class G right up to 700 or 1,200 feet above the wheat.`],
        terms: [[`Controlled airspace`,`Classes A, B, C, D, and E. ATC provides service according to the class.`],[`Class A`,`18,000 feet MSL up to and including FL600. IFR only. Not charted on VFR charts.`],[`Class E`,`Controlled airspace that isn't A through D. Floors at 14,500 MSL by default, often 1,200 or 700 AGL, sometimes the surface.`],[`Class G`,`Uncontrolled airspace from the surface to the base of the overlying Class E.`],[`Vignette`,`The shaded band on a sectional marking a Class E floor. Magenta means 700 AGL. Blue means a different floor bordering Class G.`]],
        exam: `Know the vertical limits of each class and how each one is drawn. Class E floors at 700 AGL (magenta vignette), 1,200 AGL (blue vignette next to Class G), the surface (dashed magenta), or 14,500 MSL where nothing else is charted.` },
      { h: `Class B: the upside down wedding cake`,
        body: [`Item 1 on your list. Class B surrounds the busiest airports in the country, and it's built in layers. A surface area around the main airport, then shelves that get wider and higher as you go out, so the whole thing looks like a wedding cake somebody flipped over. The AIM really does call it that. It's shaped that way to keep the airliners inside it all the way down to the runway.`,
          `Each layer gets a blue fraction on the chart. The top number is the ceiling and the bottom number is the floor, both in hundreds of feet MSL. So 80 on the bottom means the floor of that shelf is 8,000 feet above sea level. Not above the ground. Sea level. Now look where that 80 sits. Right over us. The FAA's own Centennial pilot page says it in one sentence: the base of the Denver Class B over Centennial is 8,000. Our field is 5,885. Do the subtraction. That's about 2,100 feet of sky between our runways and the bottom of the most regulated airspace in the region. You'll be climbing through that in under five minutes every single time you leave here, and the shelf is sitting right there waiting for you to get distracted.`,
          `Here's the rule that matters most in this whole chapter. You need an ATC clearance to enter Class B. Every time, no matter the weather. That's 91.131(a)(1). And a clearance has words. The controller will say your call sign and cleared to enter the Bravo airspace, or cleared through the Bravo airspace. If you didn't hear those words, you aren't cleared. Radar contact is not a clearance. A squawk code is not a clearance. Flight following is not a clearance. Being handed off from the tower to Approach and having a nice conversation with them is not a clearance. And if ATC gives you a heading or an altitude that would put you inside without saying the words, you say so. Their own rulebook says a VFR pilot is expected to speak up if an instruction would make them break a regulation. If you're not sure, you're not cleared. Ask.`,
          `To fly in it at all, the airplane needs a two way radio, a transponder with Mode C altitude reporting, and ADS-B Out. The pilot needs at least a private certificate, or, for a student, 61.95. That means ground and flight training from an instructor on that specific Class B, with the flight training done in it, and a logbook endorsement from that instructor, dated within the 90 days before the flight, saying you're proficient to solo in that specific Class B. Taking off or landing at an airport inside Class B takes its own endorsement for that airport. And there's a list of twelve airports in Appendix D, section 4 where solo students can't take off or land at all, period. Atlanta, O'Hare, LAX, the New York ones, a few others. Denver International isn't on it. You still aren't going there.`,
          `VFR inside Class B is 3 miles visibility and clear of clouds, and ATC separates you from other traffic while you're in it. Under a Class B shelf, or in a VFR corridor through one, the speed limit is 200 knots indicated, 91.117(c). Everywhere below 10,000 MSL it's 250. Biscuit couldn't do 200 if you dropped her off a cliff. The test asks anyway.`,
          `One more thing from the AIM, and I want you to take it personally. It cautions VFR pilots against flying too close to Class B boundaries, especially where the floor is low or close to the altitudes people like to cruise at. That's us. My rule is I stay at least 300 feet under any floor I'm not cleared into. Make it your rule too, until you make a better one.`],
        terms: [[`Class B`,`Layered controlled airspace around the busiest airports, surface to a charted ceiling. ATC clearance required to enter.`],[`Class B fraction`,`Blue figures on the chart. Top is the ceiling, bottom is the floor, in hundreds of feet MSL.`],[`61.95 endorsement`,`Required for a student to solo in a specific Class B. Training on that airspace, in that airspace, endorsed within the preceding 90 days.`],[`Clearance`,`Explicit permission from ATC. For Class B, the words are cleared to enter or cleared through the Bravo airspace. Anything vaguer, ask.`]],
        exam: `Class B needs a clearance, a two way radio, a Mode C transponder, and ADS-B Out. VFR minimums are 3 SM and clear of clouds. Students need a 61.95 endorsement within 90 days. The speed limit under a shelf is 200 knots.` },
      { h: `The veil, the transponder, and ADS-B Out`,
        body: [`Item 3. That thin magenta circle is the Mode C veil. It's 30 nautical miles around every airport listed in Appendix D, section 1 of Part 91, which is mostly the Class B airports, and Denver International is on the list. Inside it, from the surface up to 10,000 feet MSL, every airplane needs a transponder with altitude reporting and ADS-B Out. That's 91.215(b)(2) and 91.225(d)(2). We're about 20 miles from Denver International. We are in the veil sitting on the ramp with the engine off.`,
          `The same equipment is required in Class A, B, and C, and above the ceiling of a Class B or C area within its lateral limits, up to 10,000 MSL. And everywhere in the lower 48 at and above 10,000 MSL, except within 2,500 feet of the ground. That exception matters out here. Out past the veil, climb to 10,500 over the foothills, and if the terrain under you is within 2,500 feet, the 10,000 foot rule doesn't apply right there. ADS-B adds one area the transponder rule doesn't have: over the Gulf, at and above 3,000 MSL, out to 12 miles from the coast. The one exemption is for aircraft that were never certificated with an engine driven electrical system, plus balloons and gliders. They can be in the veil as long as they stay out of the Class B and C and below their ceilings or 10,000, whichever is lower. Biscuit has an alternator, so that's not us.`,
          `Transponders answer radar. ADS-B Out is different. It's the airplane broadcasting its own GPS position and velocity, over and over, to anybody listening, ground stations and other airplanes. It has to transmit at all times, 91.225(f), unless ATC tells you otherwise. Below 18,000 feet either of the two approved systems is legal. Class A requires the 1090 megahertz version. Marisol and Danny installed Biscuit's box in 2019, before the January 1, 2020 deadline, and Marisol will tell you how many weekends that took if you buy her lunch.`,
          `VFR, you squawk 1200 unless ATC gives you something else, with altitude reporting on. 7700 is an emergency. 7600 means you've lost your radio. If something's broken, there are deviation rules: if the transponder or ADS-B quits, you can ask ATC at any time to fly to your destination or to a repair shop. If the airplane isn't equipped at all, you ask at least an hour before. The AIM says the ADS-B request goes through an FAA web tool, between one and 24 hours ahead. And any of those is permission you ask for. It isn't something you get to assume.`],
        terms: [[`Mode C veil`,`30 NM around Appendix D section 1 airports, surface to 10,000 MSL. Transponder with altitude reporting and ADS-B Out required.`],[`ADS-B Out`,`Equipment that broadcasts the aircraft's GPS position and velocity. Must transmit at all times when installed, unless ATC directs otherwise.`],[`1200`,`The VFR transponder code.`],[`7600`,`Transponder code for lost two way radio communication.`]],
        exam: `Transponder with Mode C and ADS-B Out: Class A, B, C, the 30 NM veil to 10,000 MSL, above B and C ceilings to 10,000, and at and above 10,000 MSL in the 48 states except within 2,500 AGL. 1200 VFR, 7600 lost comm, 7700 emergency.` },
      { h: `Class C and Class D: say my name`,
        body: [`Class C first, even though we don't have one. Colorado Springs does. It's typically a 5 mile core from the surface up to 4,000 feet above the airport, and a 10 mile shelf from 1,200 feet up to 4,000 above the airport, all charted in MSL and drawn in solid magenta. There's also an outer area, normally 20 miles, that isn't charted. There's no pilot certificate requirement. You need a two way radio, a Mode C transponder, and ADS-B Out, and you need to establish two way radio communication before you go in. Inside, ATC separates VFR traffic from IFR traffic.`,
          `Class D is what we live in. It's usually from the surface up to 2,500 feet above the airport, charted in MSL, around an airport with an operating control tower. It's the dashed blue line, item 2 on your list, and the little box is the ceiling in hundreds of feet. The minus sign means up to but not including. So minus 80 means our Class D runs from the surface up to but not including 8,000 MSL, which is exactly where the Class B floor starts. They stacked them like Legos. Centennial's tower runs 24 hours. Some don't. Rocky Mountain Metro's tower runs six in the morning until ten at night, and when a part time tower closes, the airspace turns into Class E or Class G. The Chart Supplement says which.`,
          `Now the rule for C and D, and it's a sneaky one. You need two way radio communication established before you enter. Established means the controller answered you with your call sign. Cessna Four Seven Niner One Echo, standby: you're established, you can go in. Aircraft calling Centennial Tower, standby: you are not, because they didn't say who they were talking to. Cessna Niner One Echo, remain outside the Class Delta: you're established and you've been told to stay out, so you stay out. That's straight from the AIM. The test loves it, and so do I.`,
          `At any airport with an operating tower, you need a clearance to taxi, take off, or land, 91.129(i). At or below 2,500 feet above the ground within 4 miles of the main airport of a Class C or D, the speed limit is 200 knots. And if you're flying to a towered airport that sits in Class E or G, you still have to talk to the tower, and 91.126 and 91.127 say you establish that before you're 4 miles out, up to and including 2,500 feet above the ground. One more thing about Class D: the tower sequences you to the runway, but it doesn't separate VFR traffic for you. Your eyes do that.`],
        terms: [[`Class C`,`Usually a 5 NM core from the surface and a 10 NM shelf from 1,200 AGL, both to 4,000 AGL. Radio contact, Mode C, and ADS-B Out required.`],[`Class D`,`Usually surface to 2,500 AGL around a towered airport. Two way radio communication required before entry.`],[`Established communication`,`The controller has responded using your call sign. "Aircraft calling, standby" doesn't count.`],[`Minus sign on a Class D ceiling`,`Up to but not including that altitude, usually because Class B or C sits on top.`]],
        exam: `If the controller uses your call sign, communication is established, even with "standby." If they don't, or they say remain outside, you stay out. Class C and D speed limit within 4 NM, at or below 2,500 AGL: 200 knots.` },
      { h: `How far from the clouds, and how far you can see`,
        body: [`Now 91.155, basic VFR weather minimums. This is the table everybody memorizes and then forgets on a gray morning. Visibility is how far you can see ahead. Cloud clearance is how far you stay from the clouds, below, above, and sideways. Here it is.`,
          `Class A doesn't apply, you're not there. Class B is 3 statute miles and clear of clouds. Class C, Class D, and Class E below 10,000 MSL are all the same: 3 miles, and 500 feet below the clouds, 1,000 feet above, 2,000 feet horizontal. People call that three one five two. Three miles, one thousand above, five hundred below, two thousand sideways. Class E at or above 10,000 MSL gets stricter because everybody's going faster up there: 5 miles, 1,000 below, 1,000 above, and a mile horizontal.`,
          `Class G has three bands, and the test lives in them. At or below 1,200 feet above the surface, no matter the MSL altitude, it's 1 mile and clear of clouds by day, and three one five two at night. More than 1,200 above the surface but below 10,000 MSL, it's 1 mile by day and 3 at night, and both use the 500 below, 1,000 above, 2,000 horizontal clearances. More than 1,200 above the surface and at or above 10,000 MSL, it's 5 miles and 1,000, 1,000, and a mile, same as Class E up there. There's one night exception for airplanes in Class G under 1,200 AGL: in a traffic pattern within half a mile of the runway, you can go clear of clouds with 1 mile.`,
          `Think about where we live. Field elevation is 5,885. Ten thousand MSL is barely 4,100 feet over the airport. Over the mountains, you hit the 10,000 foot rules before you've cleared the foothills.`,
          `Two more out of 91.155, both about airports. You can't fly VFR beneath a ceiling of less than 1,000 feet within the surface area of controlled airspace. And you can't take off, land, or enter the pattern in a Class B, C, D, or E surface area unless the ground visibility is at least 3 miles. If there's no ground visibility reported, then the flight visibility has to be 3. Our Class D is a surface area. So two and a half miles in smoke, and nobody from this airport is leaving VFR.`,
          `Unless they ask for Special VFR. That's 91.157. Inside the surface area, below 10,000 MSL, with an ATC clearance, you can go clear of clouds with 1 mile of flight visibility, and you need 1 mile of ground visibility to take off or land. At night it's only for instrument rated pilots in airplanes equipped for IFR. It isn't allowed at all at the airports in Appendix D, section 3, and Denver International is one of them. That's your item 5, NO SVFR over the airport name. I'll be honest about Special VFR. It's legal, and it's also how good pilots end up running into clouds they couldn't see coming at a mile. Biscuit and I don't do it with students.`],
        terms: [[`Three one five two`,`3 SM, 1,000 feet above, 500 below, 2,000 horizontal. Class C, D, and E below 10,000 MSL.`],[`Class B minimums`,`3 SM and clear of clouds.`],[`Class G day, 1,200 AGL or less`,`1 SM and clear of clouds.`],[`91.155(d)`,`3 SM ground visibility to take off, land, or enter the pattern in a surface area under VFR.`],[`Special VFR`,`ATC clearance, clear of clouds, 1 SM flight visibility, within a surface area below 10,000 MSL. Night only for instrument rated pilots in IFR equipped aircraft.`]],
        exam: `Expect the table, especially Class G by day and night and the change at 10,000 MSL. Expect 91.155(c) and (d) and the Special VFR requirements. Know the 1,200 AGL break is measured above the surface.` },
      { h: `Special use airspace, and the other stuff on the chart`,
        body: [`Special use airspace is where something's going on that the rest of us need to stay out of or watch out for. Two kinds are regulatory, written into Part 73. Prohibited areas, marked P and a number, you don't fly in. Period. The PHAK uses P-40 at Camp David. Restricted areas, R and a number, have invisible hazards like artillery, aerial gunnery, and missiles. When they're active, you don't go in without permission from the using or controlling agency, 91.133. When they're inactive and released to the FAA, IFR traffic can get routed through.`,
          `The rest are nonregulatory, which means warnings, and you ignore them at your own risk. Warning areas, W, start 3 miles off the coast and cover the same kind of hazards out over the water. Military operations areas have names, not numbers, and they're there to keep military training away from IFR traffic. VFR, you're allowed in one, but if it's active you can meet fighters doing aerobatics at well over 250 knots, so you call the controlling agency or any Flight Service station within 100 miles for the status. Alert areas, A, mean lots of training or unusual flying, and everybody in there is equally responsible for not hitting each other. Controlled firing areas aren't charted at all, because they stop shooting the second anyone sees an airplane coming. National security areas ask you to avoid them voluntarily, and they can be made temporarily prohibited by NOTAM when the government needs that. The chart margins list the special use areas with their altitudes, hours, and who controls them.`,
          `Then other airspace. Military training routes are low, fast highways for military jets, mostly below 10,000 MSL at speeds over 250 knots. IR routes are flown IFR, VR routes VFR. Four digits means no segment of it goes above 1,500 feet AGL. Three digits means at least one segment does. Terminal radar service areas are drawn with a black outline, and using them is voluntary. There are parachute jump areas, published VFR routes, and VFR flyways on the back of terminal area charts. You'll see some of each before you're done.`],
        terms: [[`Prohibited area`,`P followed by a number. Flight is prohibited. Regulatory, Part 73.`],[`Restricted area`,`R followed by a number. Invisible hazards. Permission needed from the using or controlling agency when active.`],[`MOA`,`Military operations area. Named. Separates military training from IFR traffic. VFR may enter with caution.`],[`Alert area`,`A followed by a number. High volume training or unusual activity. All pilots share collision avoidance responsibility.`],[`MTR numbering`,`Four digits: no segment above 1,500 AGL. Three digits: at least one segment above 1,500 AGL.`]],
        exam: `Match each special use area to its letter and what it means for a VFR pilot. Prohibited and restricted are regulatory. Know the MTR numbering rule and that CFAs aren't charted.` },
      { h: `TFRs and NOTAMs: the airspace that isn't on the chart`,
        body: [`Your sectional is printed every 56 days. The sky changes faster than that. NOTAM is short for Notice to Airmen, and it's how the FAA tells you about everything temporary: closed runways, broken navaids, cranes, airshows, and airspace that didn't exist yesterday. 91.103 says you become familiar with all available information before a flight, and NOTAMs are information. You'll get them from Flight Service, from NOTAM Search on the FAA's site, or from a flight planning app that pulls them in.`,
          `There are a few classes. NOTAM D is the everyday kind, for airports, runways, taxiways, lighting, navaids, and general airspace activity. FDC NOTAMs are regulatory: changes to instrument procedures and charts, high barometric pressure warnings, laser activity, and temporary flight restrictions. Pointer NOTAMs point to other NOTAMs. There are international and military ones too.`,
          `A TFR is issued as an FDC NOTAM, and there are rules behind each type. 91.137 covers disasters and hazards. Under (a)(1), it's a hazard on the surface, a gas leak, say, and only relief aircraft under the direction of the official in charge get to go in. Under (a)(2), it's to give disaster relief aircraft room to work, like air tankers on a fire. There's a short list of who else can be in there: aircraft carrying law enforcement, IFR flights approved by ATC, flights going straight to or from an airport inside the area, or that have to cross it because weather or terrain makes going around impracticable, after notifying the facility named in the NOTAM and never to look at the disaster, and accredited news media with a flight plan, flying above the relief aircraft. Sightseers are not on the list. Under (a)(3), it's to keep sightseers from piling up over something newsworthy. 91.141 is the President, the Vice President, and other public figures. 91.143 is space launches. 91.144 is barometric pressure over 31 inches. 91.145 covers airshows and major sporting events. For an aerial demonstration it's normally 5 miles around, and for a sporting event 3 miles and 2,500 feet above the surface, with the NOTAM out at least 30 days ahead.`,
          `Here's the trap. Only national security TFRs are drawn on the sectional. The rest you have to go look up, every time. The PHAK says it plainly: pilots who bust TFRs have ended up in security investigations and with certificate suspensions. When you read one, find the center point, the radius, the altitudes, the times in Zulu, who's in charge, and who to call. Then draw it on your chart in pencil and give it room.`],
        terms: [[`NOTAM`,`Notice to Airmen. Time critical, temporary, or not yet charted information.`],[`FDC NOTAM`,`A regulatory NOTAM. TFRs, instrument procedure changes, and similar items.`],[`TFR`,`Temporary flight restriction, issued by FDC NOTAM under 91.137 through 91.145.`],[`91.137(a)(2)`,`TFR to give disaster relief aircraft, such as firefighting aircraft, a safe place to work.`]],
        exam: `TFRs are FDC NOTAMs. Know which section covers which kind. Most TFRs are not charted, so checking NOTAMs is part of preflight under 91.103.` },
      { h: `Talking on the radio`,
        body: [`The radio scares everybody for about ten flights. Then it's just a phone call where everybody talks too fast. The AIM gives you the format for a first call to anybody: who you're calling, who you are, where you are, and what you want. Centennial's own FAA page says what Ground wants from a VFR airplane: call sign, type, that you're VFR, the direction you want to go, and the ATIS letter. So it sounds like this. Centennial Ground, Cessna Four Seven Niner One Echo, at Hangar Six, VFR eastbound, with Lima. That's it. They'll do the rest.`,
          `Your call sign is the type and the numbers. When you say Cessna, you drop the N. You never shorten it on the first call. After the controller shortens it, you can too, and the short version is the type and the last three, Cessna Niner One Echo. If two call signs on the frequency sound alike, use the whole thing and make them use it too.`,
          `Numbers. Nine is niner. Altitudes are said in thousands and hundreds, seven thousand five hundred, and anything at ten thousand or more gets the digits split, one zero thousand five hundred. A decimal in a frequency is point. Headings are three digits, heading zero niner zero. Time on the radio is Zulu unless somebody says local.`,
          `Read back runway assignments, hold short instructions, and anything with a number in it. Controllers are required to get a readback of every runway hold short instruction, so you might as well give it to them the first time. Two phrases at Centennial get people in trouble. Monitor Tower means switch to the tower frequency and listen. It does not mean call them, and it is not a clearance onto the runway. And line up and wait means taxi onto the runway and sit there. It is not a takeoff clearance. Taxi instructions to a runway don't let you onto that runway either. You hold short until somebody tells you otherwise.`,
          `Last one. Sooner or later a controller may say this to you: your call sign, then possible pilot deviation, advise you contact, then a facility, at, then a phone number. Pilots call it the Brasher warning, after a court case. It's in the controllers' rulebook word for word. It means they think you broke a rule, and they're giving you a chance to write it down while it's fresh. If you ever hear it, fly the airplane first. Acknowledge it, write the number down, and get on the ground. Don't argue on frequency. Then calm down and call. Then file a report with NASA's Aviation Safety Reporting System. Under AC 00-46F, if you get that report in within 10 days after the event, or after you realized it happened, and it was inadvertent, didn't involve a crime or an accident, and you haven't had a violation in the last five years, the FAA can still find a violation but it won't fine you or suspend your certificate. Walt has forms. Of course Walt has forms.`],
        terms: [[`Initial contact`,`Facility, full call sign, position, request. Add the ATIS letter when you have it.`],[`Abbreviated call sign`,`Type plus the last three characters, used only after the controller abbreviates first.`],[`Monitor Tower`,`Switch to the tower frequency and listen. It isn't a request to call and it isn't a clearance.`],[`Brasher warning`,`"Possible pilot deviation, advise you contact (facility) at (number)." ATC phraseology from JO 7110.65.`],[`ASRS report`,`A report to NASA. Filed within 10 days, it can waive a civil penalty or suspension for an inadvertent violation under AC 00-46F.`]],
        exam: `Expect a question on the order of an initial call, on abbreviating call signs, on what "line up and wait" allows, and on the protection a timely NASA report gives you.` },
      { h: `When the radio quits: light gun signals`,
        body: [`Every tower has a light gun, a big spotlight that can shine red, green, or white at one airplane. It's for airplanes with no radio, or airplanes whose radio quit. It's in 91.125, and you're going to memorize it because the test will absolutely ask.`,
          `On the ground: steady green is cleared for takeoff. Flashing green is cleared to taxi. Steady red is stop. Flashing red is taxi clear of the runway in use. Flashing white is return to your starting point on the airport. In the air: steady green is cleared to land. Flashing green is return for landing, and a steady green will follow when it's time. Steady red is give way to other aircraft and keep circling. Flashing red is airport unsafe, do not land. Flashing white in the air doesn't mean anything. Alternating red and green, anywhere, is exercise extreme caution.`,
          `In the air by day, rock your wings. On the ground by day, move your ailerons or rudder. At night, either way, blink your landing or nav lights.`,
          `If you lose the radio in our Class D, 91.129(d) says you can still come in and land if the weather's at or above basic VFR minimums, you keep visual contact with the tower, and you get a clearance to land, which here means a steady green. Squawk 7600 so the radar people know what's going on. And before you decide the radio is dead, check the dumb stuff: the volume, the squelch, the headset plugs, the frequency you dialed, the push to talk switch. Half of the radio failures I've had were me.`],
        terms: [[`Steady green`,`On the ground, cleared for takeoff. In the air, cleared to land.`],[`Flashing green`,`On the ground, cleared to taxi. In the air, return for landing.`],[`Steady red`,`On the ground, stop. In the air, give way to other aircraft and continue circling.`],[`Flashing red`,`On the ground, taxi clear of the runway in use. In the air, airport unsafe, do not land.`],[`Flashing white`,`On the ground only: return to the starting point on the airport.`]],
        exam: `The full light gun table, ground and air. Flashing white has no meaning in flight. Lost comm VFR into Class D: VFR weather, visual contact with the tower, and a clearance to land.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f4s1`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.E`, title:`Eighty`, q:`Over Centennial, the sectional shows a blue 80. What does it tell Jordan?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Saturday at seven the briefing room smelled like burnt coffee and the dry erase markers somebody had left uncapped all week. Abby had taped Jordan's new sectional to the wall over the whiteboard, flat, with blue painter's tape at the corners. She didn't say good morning. She tapped the blue 80 over Centennial with a pen.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"This one. What is it, and what does it mean for us in about four minutes after takeoff?"</span>`,`Jordan had Danny's old chart in her flight bag, folded to the same panel, with the same number circled in red three times. She didn't take it out.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`The Class B floor is 8,000 MSL, and at or above it she needs a clearance.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Yep. Eight thousand above sea level, which is about twenty one hundred feet over our heads right now."</span> She draws a line on the whiteboard for the runway, then a lid over it. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Climb out east, look at a pretty reservoir, look back at your altimeter, and you're inside the most expensive airspace in Colorado. I've seen students do it in a Cessna with an instructor sitting right there. I've seen instructors do it."</span>`,
          why:`Class B floors and ceilings are in hundreds of feet MSL, bottom number floor, top number ceiling. Over Centennial the floor is 8,000 MSL, only about 2,100 feet above the field. Entry at or above it takes an ATC clearance under 91.131.`},
        {t:`The Class B starts 8,000 feet above the ground, with plenty of room under it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Plenty of room."</span> She writes 5,885 on the board and adds 8,000 to it. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"That puts the floor at fourteen thousand. The airliners would love that. It's MSL. Always MSL on these."</span>`,
          why:`Class B altitudes on a sectional are MSL. Reading them as AGL here puts the floor almost 6,000 feet too high, which is how a student climbs right into the shelf thinking she's under it.`},
        {t:`The Class B floor is 8,000 MSL, and at or above it she must be talking to Approach.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Talking to them isn't enough."</span> She underlines the word on the board: CLEARED. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"You can talk to Approach all day long and still be busting their airspace. Talking is Class C. Bravo needs the words."</span>`,
          why:`Right altitude, wrong requirement. Two way communication is the rule for Class C and D. Class B needs an explicit ATC clearance to enter, and being in contact with Approach doesn't give you one.`},
        {t:`The Class B ends at 8,000 feet, and above that, they can fly freely.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Upside down. Bottom number's the floor."</span> She points to the top number in the same blue fraction. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"That's where it ends. You're living underneath this thing, not on top of it."</span>`,
          why:`In a Class B fraction the top figure is the ceiling and the bottom figure is the floor. The 80 is the floor, so the airspace starts at 8,000 MSL over the field and goes up from there.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s2`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.A`, title:`Ninety days`, q:`Under 14 CFR 61.95, can a student pilot ever solo in Denver's Class B?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Abby went through the whole cake, layer by layer, drawing it from the side on the whiteboard in blue marker until it looked like a stack of pancakes somebody had dropped. Jordan asked the question she'd been holding since Tuesday.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"After I solo. Could I ever fly up there by myself? Through the Bravo, over Denver International. Just to see it."</span>`,`Abby capped the marker and looked at her for a second, like she was deciding which answer to give and then deciding to give the real one.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`No. A student can't solo in any Class B until she's certificated.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Not banned. There are twelve airports where you can't take off or land solo. That's a different rule, and this isn't one of them."</span> She writes 61.95 on the board. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"There's a way. It's work, and it's mine to sign off, and I'm stingy."</span>`,
          why:`There's an outright ban on solo student takeoffs and landings, but only at the airports in Appendix D, section 4, like Atlanta and LAX. Elsewhere, 61.95 lets a student solo in a specific Class B with the right training and a current endorsement.`},
        {t:`Yes, once Denver Approach clears her in, with nothing else required.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"The clearance is the thing ATC needs. The FAA needs a bunch more from you."</span> She writes PILOT REQUIREMENTS and underlines it twice.`,
          why:`A clearance is required, and it isn't the only requirement. 91.131(b) also says who can be the pilot, and for a student that means meeting 61.95 first.`},
        {t:`Yes, once she's passed the knowledge test and knows the airspace rules.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"The written proves you can pick C out of three. That's it."</span>`,
          why:`The knowledge test has nothing to do with Class B privileges. A student needs specific training and a current, specific endorsement under 61.95, or a private certificate.`},
        {t:`Yes, with training in it and an instructor endorsement within 90 days.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Right. Ground and flight training on Denver's Bravo, the flying done in it, and my signature in your logbook within the last ninety days saying you can solo in it. Taking off or landing at an airport inside it takes another one for that airport."</span> She smiles, which is rare enough that Jordan notices. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Danny asked me the same thing once, years after his checkride. I wasn't his instructor. I told him to go buy his instructor a burrito."</span>`,
          why:`61.95(a): ground and flight training from an authorized instructor on that specific Class B, flight training received in it, and a logbook endorsement dated within the preceding 90 days for solo in that airspace. 61.95(b) adds a separate endorsement for an airport inside it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s3`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.E`, title:`The veil`, q:`The ADS-B annunciator reads FAULT on a perfect morning under the Mode C veil. What should Jordan do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Sunday morning, before a lesson, Jordan turned the master on for the preflight and a small amber light lit up on the panel next to the transponder. ADS-B. The label under it, in Marisol's label maker capitals, said FAULT.`,`Marisol was across the ramp with her head in someone else's cowling. Abby's text said she was fifteen minutes out. The sky was perfect, a calm blue morning, the windsock hanging straight down like it had given up on life.`,`Jordan looked at the light. The transponder itself was fine. It showed 1200 and the little reply light blinked when she pressed the test.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Fly the lesson. The transponder works and reports altitude, and that covers the veil.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`Abby, when she arrives, reads the light before she reads Jordan's face. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Transponder's half of it. The veil wants both."</span>`,
          why:`Inside the 30 NM Mode C veil, from the surface to 10,000 MSL, 91.215 requires the transponder with altitude reporting and 91.225 requires ADS-B Out. One working doesn't excuse the other.`},
        {t:`Stay below 8,000 and out of the Class B, where the ADS-B rule doesn't apply.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby" data-tone="Flat, dry, deliberate.">"The veil goes all the way to the ground."</span> She traces the magenta circle on the chart with a finger. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"We're inside it parked."</span>`,
          why:`The ADS-B Out requirement within 30 NM of Denver International starts at the surface, not at the Class B floor. Staying under the shelf doesn't get you out of the veil.`},
        {t:`Show Marisol the light, and keep Biscuit on the ground until it's fixed.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:5},
          after:`Marisol comes over wiping her hands, looks at the light, and sighs through her nose. Ten minutes later she's on her back under the panel with a flashlight in her teeth. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"GPS antenna connector. Backed off. The box can't tell anybody where it is if it doesn't know where it is."</span> She reseats it, safety wires it, and the light goes out. Then she sits on the wing for a minute. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Danny and I put this thing in the fall of 2019. Every Saturday in October. He kept saying they'd push the deadline. They didn't."</span> They're wheels up forty minutes late. Abby doesn't mention it.`,
          why:`ADS-B Out is required in the veil, and an annunciated fault means you don't know what the airplane is broadcasting. Ground it, tell the mechanic, and fly when it's fixed. Inoperative equipment that a rule requires can't be deferred with a placard.`},
        {t:`Call Denver Approach and ask for a deviation to fly today's lesson without it.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Abby, on the phone: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"There's a deviation for flying a broken box to the shop or to where you were going. A lesson that ends where it started isn't really that. Show Marisol."</span>`,
          why:`91.225(g) lets you ask ATC to fly with an inoperative ADS-B Out to your destination or to a repair shop, so the instinct to ask is sound. But using it to go do a training flight when the mechanic is fifty yards away is the wrong tool.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s4`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`III.A`, title:`Ground, Cessna`, q:`Which initial call to Centennial Ground has everything the AIM format asks for?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The next Saturday was all radio. Abby said so on the walk out. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"You make every call today. Ground, Tower, Approach, all of it. I'm going to sit here and be a passenger with opinions."</span>`,`Jordan had the ATIS written on her kneeboard: Information Lima, wind calm, runway 35R in use. She had a script written under it, then crossed it out, then written it again. Her thumb was on the push to talk switch. On a medical channel she could talk a crew through a cardiac arrest without thinking. She had no idea why this was harder.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Whenever you're ready. They're not going anywhere."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"Centennial Ground, Cessna Niner One Echo, ready to taxi to the active runway."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Ground answers with a question. <span class="said" data-who="man">"Cessna calling, say full call sign, position, and do you have the ATIS?"</span> Jordan feels her face go hot. Abby studies a cloud.`,
          why:`Never abbreviate your call sign on the first contact. The controller also needs your position, your direction of flight, and the ATIS letter, so this call just started a conversation instead of finishing one.`},
        {t:`"Centennial Ground, Cessna Four Seven Niner One Echo at Hangar Six, VFR eastbound with Lima."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="man">"Cessna Four Seven Niner One Echo, Centennial Ground, runway three five right, taxi via Alpha. Advise run up complete."</span> She reads it back, the runway and the taxiway and the call sign, and he says <span class="said" data-who="man">"readback correct"</span> like it's nothing. Like she does this. Abby gives her a thumbs up without looking over, which somehow counts more.`,
          why:`Who you're calling, your full call sign, where you are, what you want, and the ATIS. It's the AIM format, and it's what Centennial's FAA page says Ground wants from a VFR airplane. One clean call, one clean answer.`},
        {t:`"Ground, this is Biscuit at Hangar Six, we're a student pilot, we'd like to go east today."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="man">"Aircraft calling Centennial Ground, say again your call sign?"</span> Abby, on the intercom, very quietly: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Biscuit's her name. Not her call sign."</span>`,
          why:`Nicknames aren't call signs. The controller needs the type and registration to know who you are and to match you to the radar and the ground track. Lead with the facility, then the call sign.`},
        {t:`"Centennial Ground, Cessna Four Seven Niner One Echo, at Hangar Six, ready to taxi."`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="man">"Cessna Four Seven Niner One Echo, say direction of flight, and verify you have Lima."</span> She gives it. It works. It just took two calls to do the job of one.`,
          why:`Good structure and the full call sign. But Ground wants your direction of flight and the ATIS letter on the first call, so they can plan your taxi and know you have the weather. Leave those out and they'll ask.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s5`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`III.A`, title:`Monitor`, q:`What does Ground's instruction to monitor Tower ask of Jordan?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The run up went fine. Jordan called it complete. Ground came back fast.`,`<span class="said" data-who="man">"Cessna Niner One Echo, hold short runway three five right at Alpha, monitor Tower."</span>`,`She read back the hold short, dialed the tower frequency off her kneeboard, switched over, and rolled up to the hold short line. Four yellow stripes, two solid and two dashed, solid on her side. Tower was a mess. A jet checking in from the north. A helicopter with a long request about the reservoir. Some guy asking about fuel prices, on tower, like it was a drive thru. The runway just sat there in front of her, empty, pointed at the mountains. Nobody said Niner One Echo.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Call Tower and tell them she's holding short of three five right at Alpha, ready to go.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`A pause on frequency. <span class="said" data-who="man">"Cessna Niner One Echo, I have you. Continue holding."</span> Abby: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Monitor means they'll call you. They already knew you were there."</span>`,
          why:`Not dangerous, and plenty of pilots do it. But Monitor Tower means listen. Centennial's FAA page says it plainly: monitor does not mean to call. Ground already handed you over, so the extra call just uses airtime on a busy frequency.`},
        {t:`Taxi onto the runway and line up. Monitor means the tower's planning to clear her next.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`Her hand's on the throttle when Abby's lands on top of it, not hard. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Nobody said line up. Nobody said cleared. And there's a jet on final."</span> Four seconds later a Citation goes by the windshield with its wheels hanging, close enough that Jordan reads the tail number and then can't remember a single digit of it.`,
          why:`An instruction to monitor the tower is not a clearance onto the runway or for takeoff. Without "line up and wait" or "cleared for takeoff," you stay behind the hold short line. Entering a runway without a clearance is how collisions happen.`},
        {t:`Hold short and listen on the tower frequency until the tower calls Niner One Echo.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`She sits on the brakes. Two minutes, about. A Citation lands and turns off. A Cirrus goes off the left side. Then: <span class="said" data-who="man">"Cessna Niner One Echo, runway three five right, cleared for takeoff, right turn eastbound approved."</span> She reads it back a little too fast, lines up, and pushes the throttle in. Door pops at rotation, like always. She's latched it and is trimming for the climb before it occurs to her that her hand did that on its own.`,
          why:`Monitor means switch frequencies and listen. It's not a request to call and it's not a clearance. Ground told you to hold short, you read it back, and the tower will call you when it wants you.`},
        {t:`Stay on Ground's frequency until somebody there tells her to switch over.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`Ground's got a fuel truck and a Bonanza that can't find Alpha. Abby gives it a minute. Then she reaches over and does it herself. Tower's already talking, and it's saying Jordan's call sign. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"They've been calling you."</span> They had. Twice.`,
          why:`Monitor Tower is an instruction to change frequencies. If you stay on Ground, the tower can't reach you, and they'll be calling a pilot who isn't listening.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s6`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`III.A`, title:`Aircraft calling`, q:`Tower said standby, but didn't name anyone. What should Jordan do before she reaches the Class D?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`After an hour over the plains the radio had stopped being terrifying and started being annoying. Abby said that was the correct order. Coming home, she listened to the ATIS, wrote it down, and called the tower about twelve miles east of the airport, outside the dashed blue line, at 7,500, under the shelf.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Centennial Tower, Cessna Four Seven Niner One Echo, one two miles east, seven thousand five hundred, landing with Mike."</span>`,`The reply came after a few seconds, clipped and busy.`,`<span class="said" data-who="man">"Aircraft calling Centennial Tower, standby."</span>`,`At a hundred knots the edge of the Class D was about three minutes away.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Keep heading in, with the tower's answer counting as two way contact.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`Abby taps the chart. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Who'd he say standby to?"</span> Jordan plays it back in her head. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Right. Nobody. Turn."</span>`,
          why:`The AIM is explicit: if the controller responds without your call sign, communication has not been established and you may not enter Class D. "Aircraft calling, standby" could be for anyone.`},
        {t:`Drop low and slip in under the Class D to get closer to the airport.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Under it? It starts at the ground."</span> She lets that sit. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"There's no basement."</span>`,
          why:`Class D extends upward from the surface. There's no altitude below it. Diving for the ground to sneak in trades a radio problem for a terrain problem.`},
        {t:`Turn away and stay outside the Class D until she hears her call sign.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`Left turn. Outside the dashed line. One lap over a feedlot. You can smell a feedlot from 7,500 feet, it turns out. Ninety seconds. Then: <span class="said" data-who="man">"Cessna Four Seven Niner One Echo, Centennial Tower, enter right downwind runway three five right."</span> Now she's established. She reads it back and goes in. Abby, while Jordan's busy: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Half my students hear standby and think it means come on in."</span>`,
          why:`Two way communication requires the controller to use your call sign. Until they do, stay outside the Class D. If they had said your call sign and "standby," you'd be established. If they said "remain outside," you'd stay out.`},
        {t:`Key the mic again right away and repeat her full call sign for the tower.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`She keys up on top of somebody else. Squeal. The tower: <span class="said" data-who="man">"Two stations at once. Aircraft calling, standby."</span> Abby: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"He said standby. He meant it. Stay outside and let him get to you."</span>`,
          why:`Wanting to be identified is right. But the controller told you to wait, and calling again right away clogs a busy frequency. Stay outside the Class D and let them come back to you.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s7`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.E`, title:`Two and a half miles`, q:`Centennial's METAR shows two and a half miles in smoke. What should Jordan decide?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`On the first of July a fire started in the foothills west of Sedalia, southwest of the airport, and by the second the whole south end of the metro was the color of weak tea. Jordan could smell it from her driveway in Aurora at five in the morning. The sun came up red and stayed red.`,`<prop type="doc" title="KAPA, Thursday 6:53 AM"><code>METAR KAPA 021253Z 21006KT 2 1/2SM FU SCT150 18/06 A3022 RMK AO2 SLP151 T01780061</code></prop>`,`<prop type="text" from="Lourdes Reyes" time="5:58 AM">Mija the fire is on channel 9. Tell me you are not flying in that smoke.</prop>`,`The lesson was at seven. Abby hadn't texted. Jordan had learned what that meant.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Go. Two and a half miles is close to three, and it'll be clearer out east of the smoke.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Abby's text comes back in under a minute. <b>Read 91.155(d). Then read it again.</b>`,
          why:`Within the surface area of Class B, C, D, or E airspace, you can't take off, land, or enter the pattern under VFR unless the ground visibility is at least 3 statute miles. Two and a half isn't close enough. It's below the minimum.`},
        {t:`Ask the tower for a Special VFR clearance, with its one mile visibility minimum.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:-1},
          after:`<b>Legal for some people, some days. Not for us in smoke. Smoke doesn't have an edge you can see coming.</b>`,
          why:`Special VFR under 91.157 really does allow 1 mile of visibility, clear of clouds, with a clearance, within a surface area by day. Knowing it is good. But legal and smart are different questions, and flying a student around in smoke at the bottom of the legal range isn't smart.`},
        {t:`Cancel. The field is under three miles, and 91.155(d) needs three to depart VFR.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`<b>Canceling. KAPA 2 1/2 in smoke. 91.155(d), under 3 in the surface area.</b> Abby: <b>Agree.</b> Then: <b>You quoted a regulation at me before 7 am. I'm putting that on my résumé.</b> Jordan texts her mother: <b>Not flying. Smoke. Going back to bed.</b> Three dots for a long time. Then: <b>Good girl.</b> Jordan is thirty five years old and it still lands somewhere.`,
          why:`Ground visibility under 3 SM in the Class D surface area means no VFR departure. Smoke also lowers visibility unevenly and hides terrain, other airplanes, and the fire aircraft working nearby.`},
        {t:`Drive in and wait at the airport. If the METAR goes to three miles, take off right away.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`At nine it's still 2 1/2. At ten, 3 miles exactly, and she sits in the car looking at the haze and doesn't feel like going. She texts Abby. <b>Three. Still don't love it.</b> <b>Then don't. That feeling is information too.</b>`,
          why:`Waiting for it to come up to legal minimums is legal. But a value right at the minimum in smoke isn't a margin, it's the edge. The minimum is where you stop, not where you aim.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s8`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.E`, title:`The fire`, q:`Russ swapped into Jordan's Saturday slot to show his nephew the fire under a TFR. What should Jordan do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Friday night the club chat went off like a car alarm.`,`<prop type="chat" from="Russ Kim" time="8:02 PM">swapped myself into biscuit sat 7-9. taking my nephew to see the fire, it's the coolest thing in colorado right now. jordan you can have sun, sun is better anyway</prop>`,`<prop type="chat" from="Russ Kim" time="8:03 PM">before anyone says it yes there's a tfr. it's a tfr, not a wall</prop>`,`<prop type="chat" from="Walt Herrera" time="8:09 PM">Calendar is first come. Who booked Saturday?</prop>`,`Jordan had booked Saturday on the 20th of June. She had also, an hour earlier, pulled the NOTAMs for the weekend, because now she couldn't not.`,`<prop type="doc" title="TFR, pulled Friday 7:04 PM"><code>!FDC 6/4471 ZDV CO..AIRSPACE 8NM W SEDALIA, CO..TEMPORARY FLIGHT RESTRICTIONS. PURSUANT TO 14 CFR SECTION 91.137(A)(2) TEMPORARY FLIGHT RESTRICTIONS ARE IN EFFECT WI AN AREA DEFINED AS 5NM RADIUS OF 392630N1050800W SFC-11500FT MSL TO PROVIDE A SAFE ENVIRONMENT FOR FIRE FIGHTING ACFT OPS. DOUGLAS COUNTY SHERIFF TEL 303-555-0142 IS IN CHARGE OF ON SCENE EMERG RESPONSE ACTIVITY. DENVER /ZDV/ ARTCC TEL 303-555-0188 IS THE FAA CDN FAC. 2607011800-2607122359</code></prop>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Let Russ have the Saturday slot and stay out of the TFR question.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`Russ sends a thumbs up. Walt doesn't answer. Saturday morning a little after seven, Jordan finds herself checking a flight tracking app to see where Biscuit is, and she doesn't like that she's doing it.`,
          why:`Keeping the peace is a fine instinct, but this isn't a scheduling question anymore. A 91.137(a)(2) TFR around firefighting aircraft has no exception for sightseers, and saying nothing when you know that isn't neutral.`},
        {t:`Post the NOTAM in the club chat, citing the 91.137(a)(2) limits on who can enter.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:5},
          after:`She pastes the NOTAM text in, then: <b>That's a 91.137(a)(2) for fire aircraft. Sightseeing isn't one of the exceptions. 5 mile radius, surface to 11,500. Also I booked Saturday back on June 20. Happy to trade for Sunday if you want it for something else.</b> Four minutes of nothing. Then Russ: <b>ok first of all who keeps teaching her to read.</b> Walt: <b>Still nobody.</b> Russ: <b>fine. sunday. we'll look at it from i-25 like normal people. 5 miles is a lot of miles.</b> Walt: <b>5 miles is the TFR. Give it more.</b> Marisol posts a photo of a box of donuts with a sticky note on it that says RUSS OWES.`,
          why:`The NOTAM settles the safety question and the calendar settles the schedule. Under 91.137(a)(2), only relief aircraft, law enforcement, approved IFR, flights to or from an airport inside, or a flight that has to cross it because weather or terrain makes going around impracticable, after notifying the facility in the NOTAM and never to look at the fire, and accredited news media with a flight plan, above the relief aircraft, get in. Say the facts, offer the trade, and let the rules carry the weight.`},
        {t:`Tell Russ he can go if he stays clear of the tankers and keeps his transponder on.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Walt, before Russ can answer: <b>No he can't.</b> Then nothing else from Walt for the rest of the night, which is worse.`,
          why:`A TFR isn't advice about traffic. Entering a 91.137(a)(2) area to look at a fire is a violation no matter how carefully you fly, and air tankers and helicopters working low in smoke are exactly the aircraft it protects.`},
        {t:`Text the NOTAM to Walt privately as safety officer and let him handle Russ.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Walt replies to her directly: <b>Good catch. Post it in the chat. It's your slot and your NOTAM.</b>`,
          why:`Walt should know, and going to him isn't wrong. But you found it, you're a partner, and it was your booking. Putting the facts where everyone can see them keeps it from turning into a private squabble.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s9`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`, task:`VI`, title:`Flight following`, q:`The trip to Rocky Mountain Metro winds under the Class B shelves. Which choice about ATC services fits?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The smoke cleared out on the eighth when the wind went northwest. On Saturday the 11th Abby planned a short dual cross country, Centennial to Rocky Mountain Metro and back, the long way around the west side of Denver under the Class B shelves. On the chart it looked like walking through a house in the dark where somebody keeps moving the furniture.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Your trip, your plan. After Tower lets you go, do you want flight following or not? I'm not voting."</span>`,`On the kneeboard Jordan had the shelf floors along the route written down in a column, in MSL. She'd planned to cruise at 7,500. Some of the floors were not very far above that.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Skip it. Under the shelves she doesn't need to talk to anyone, and the frequency is busy.`, s:0, flag:`noflightfollowing`, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Your call."</span> She lets Jordan fly it that way. Over Englewood a Bonanza goes by a few hundred feet above them that neither of them saw until it was going away. Abby says, in an even voice, <span class="said" data-who="Abby" data-tone="Even, flat, deliberate.">"He was on with Approach. They'd have told us about him."</span>`,
          why:`It's legal to fly under the shelves without talking to anyone. But radar traffic advisories are free, the airspace under a Class B is crowded with airplanes doing the same thing you are, and the controller can see traffic you can't. Declining them gives up a second set of eyes.`},
        {t:`Ask Tower to keep her on their frequency all the way to Rocky Mountain Metro.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Tower owns the Class D. Once you're out of it, they're going to hand you off or let you go. The en route stuff is Approach."</span>`,
          why:`Towers work their own airspace and the airport. VFR traffic advisories between airports come from the radar approach facility, so you ask Approach, or ask the tower to request it for you on departure.`},
        {t:`Request VFR traffic advisories from Denver Approach once she's out of Centennial's airspace.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`Tower hands her off. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Denver Approach, Cessna Four Seven Niner One Echo, five miles west of Centennial, six thousand eight hundred climbing seven thousand five hundred, request flight following to Rocky Mountain Metro."</span> A woman's voice gives her a squawk code. Jordan reads it back and dials it in. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Cessna Four Seven Niner One Echo, radar contact, five miles west of Centennial. Maintain VFR at or below seven thousand five hundred."</span> Over Englewood: <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Traffic, two o'clock, three miles, eastbound, a Bonanza, seven thousand niner hundred."</span> Jordan finds it. A dot that turns into an airplane. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Traffic in sight, Niner One Echo."</span> Abby writes FF on her kneeboard and draws a star next to it.`,
          why:`Flight following gives you radar traffic advisories, workload permitting, from the people who can see the traffic. It doesn't relieve you of see and avoid and it doesn't clear you into the Class B. It's a second set of eyes, and around Denver you want it.`},
        {t:`Ask Approach for a clearance through the Class B, so she can fly straight over the top.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Legal with me in the right seat. But you don't ask for a Bravo transition because the long way is annoying. Solo, you'd need my ninety day endorsement first."</span>`,
          why:`A student flying with an instructor can get a Class B clearance, and later can solo in it only with a 61.95 endorsement. But planning a route through busy Class B to save a few minutes isn't what the AIM asks for. It encourages VFR traffic to stay above or below Class B, or use the published VFR corridors.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s10`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`III.A`, title:`Steady red`, q:`What does a steady red light gun signal require of an airplane in flight?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`On the way home, over Littleton, the radio went quiet. Not static. Nothing. Approach had told them to contact Centennial Tower, she'd dialed it in and called, and nothing came back. She called again. Her own voice came back to her in the headset the way it always did, and that was all, on a frequency that hadn't shut up for one second all morning.`,`She checked the volume. The squelch. Both headset plugs. The frequency, twice, out loud. Abby tried hers. Nothing.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Okay. This is real. What now?"</span>`,`Jordan squawked 7600. They found the edge of the Class D, then the tower cab, and she joined the downwind for 35R with her head turned toward it like she was trying to hear a conversation two tables over. Abeam the numbers, a light came out of the cab at them. Red. It didn't blink.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Land anyway. She's VFR, squawking 7600, and the tower knows exactly who she is.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`Abby's hand goes to the throttle. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Red means somebody else gets to be where you want to be."</span> Twenty seconds later a King Air slides down final, big and quiet, right where Biscuit would have been.`,
          why:`91.129(d)(2) lets you land without a radio only if you're VFR, in sight of the tower, and you receive a clearance to land. A steady red in flight is the opposite of a clearance: give way to other aircraft and continue circling.`},
        {t:`Give way to other traffic and keep circling in the pattern, watching for a green.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`She stretches the downwind out past where she'd normally turn, cab in the corner of the windshield, King Air first. Then all the way around again. Nobody to talk to, so she talks to Biscuit, under her breath, which Abby pretends not to hear. On base: green. Holding. She rocks the wings so hard Abby grabs the glare shield and says a word Jordan has never heard her say, and Jordan's still grinning like a dope when the mains squeak on. Marisol has the radio out of the panel in fifteen minutes flat. Connector on the back, half off, same deal as the ADS-B antenna in June. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"This panel's been shaking itself apart all summer."</span> That night, alone, flashlight in her teeth, she goes through every plug behind it. In the morning there's a handheld radio in the seat back pocket and nobody mentions it. Later Abby tells her it was Danny's. He always kept it there. Marisol took it out in March.`,
          why:`Steady red in flight means give way to other aircraft and continue circling. Keep visual contact with the tower and wait for a steady green, which is your clearance to land. In flight by day you acknowledge by rocking your wings.`},
        {t:`Leave the pattern and divert to an airport without a tower, where no clearance is needed.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Legit plan. Not the one I'd pick with a tower right here and good weather. The light gun works. Let's use it."</span>`,
          why:`Diverting to a nontowered field is a reasonable lost comm option, and sometimes it's the best one. But in VFR weather, in sight of the tower, 91.129(d) gives you a clear way in. Steady red just means wait your turn.`},
        {t:`Read it as airport unsafe, leave the area, and try another field nearby.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"That's flashing red. This one's holding still."</span>`,
          why:`Flashing red in flight means airport unsafe, do not land. Steady red means give way and continue circling. Mixing them up means leaving an airport you could have landed at in a few minutes.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s11`, domain:`Airport and flight`, task:`III.A`, title:`A phone number`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Wednesday evening Walt came by the hangar for his monthly logbook check, the way he'd done since April. He read every line of the last month, the cancellations included, with his glasses on the end of his nose, initialed the bottom of the page, and slid the logbook back.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Abby says you're working the Bravo."</span> He set a pamphlet on the bench, the kind they hand out at safety meetings, with NASA on the front. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"A member of this club got a phone number from Denver Approach once. Climbing out east, right through the floor. Good pilot. Wasn't looking."</span>`,`Jordan waited for the name. It didn't come.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"You hear 'possible pilot deviation, advise you contact.' What do you do?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Key up, explain what happened, and give the controller her side of it now.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"On a busy frequency, with your hands full, explaining yourself to a tape recorder."</span> He shakes his head slowly. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Everything you say on that frequency is a record. Fly the airplane."</span>`,
          why:`The notification is there so you can collect your thoughts, not to start a debate on frequency. Arguing ties up the controller and puts statements on the recording that you haven't thought about yet.`},
        {t:`Fly first, call the number once she's calm on the ground, and file with NASA.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`Walt nods once. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Ten days. Within ten days of the deviation, or of when you found out. NASA form. It doesn't stop the FAA from finding you did it, but if it was an honest mistake and you're clean for five years, nobody takes your certificate or your money."</span> He taps the pamphlet. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"I've filed four in my life. Dispatch ones. Filing is what a professional does. Not filing is what somebody who thinks he's never wrong does."</span>`,
          why:`Acknowledge, fly the airplane, write the number down, get on the ground, then call. Then file an Aviation Safety Reporting System report with NASA within 10 days. Under AC 00-46F, a timely report on an inadvertent violation can waive the civil penalty or suspension.`},
        {t:`Let it go, keep flying the lesson, and wait to see if the FAA ever follows up.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`Walt takes his glasses off. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"They have the radar tape. They have your squawk and your ADS-B and your altitude every second. What they don't have yet is your attitude. Don't give them a bad one."</span>`,
          why:`The FAA has the data whether you call or not. Ignoring the notification only removes your chance to explain, and the attitude of the pilot is one of the factors the FAA weighs in enforcement under AC 00-46F.`},
        {t:`Land, then call the number from the ramp right away, before the details fade.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Calling is right. Right away, shaking, with your mouth going faster than your head, is how people talk themselves into trouble. Write it down first. Sit. Then call."</span>`,
          why:`Calling is the right move, and you should. But the warning exists to give you time to collect your thoughts. Write down what happened while it's fresh, then call. And file the NASA report.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s12`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.E`, title:`Seven thousand eight hundred`, q:`Denver Approach said radar contact, and the climb hasn't stopped. What should Jordan do now?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Saturday the 18th, clear, calm, the kind of morning that makes people buy airplanes. Abby said almost nothing on the walk out, which Jordan had learned meant a test.`,`Off 35R with a right turn east. Tower handed her to Denver Approach on the climb, and the controller gave her a squawk code. Jordan read it back, wrote it on her kneeboard, dialed it in, checked it, and the reply light blinked.`,`<span class="said" data-who="woman">"Cessna Four Seven Niner One Echo, radar contact, three miles east of Centennial."</span>`,`Good. In the system. The reservoir was off the left wing, way too blue for Colorado, and she was half thinking about the practice area and the steep turns she'd botched last week and the 7,500 she was supposed to level at for them out in the practice area. She cranked in some trim. She looked at the reservoir again, because it was right there.`,(s.flags.noflightfollowing||G.noflightfollowing)?`She was on with Approach this time. She'd made a point of it, after the Bonanza over Englewood.`:`She was on with Approach, like always. Radar contact. In the system.`,`Something moved in the corner of her eye. Abby's finger, on the glass of the altimeter, just resting there. The nail was chipped.`,`7,800. Still climbing.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Where's the shelf?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Keep climbing a little. The shelf has to step up a few miles out this way.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Has to?"</span> Then <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"My airplane,"</span> and the nose goes down and the throttle comes back, and the altimeter stops at 7,920, hangs there, and starts unwinding. For ten minutes after that Abby says nothing at all, which is worse than anything she could have said.`,
          why:`You don't climb into Class B on a guess about where the floor changes. The floor over and around Centennial is 8,000 MSL, and the clearance has to come first. Stop the climb, then find out where the shelf steps up from the chart.`},
        {t:`Level off at exactly 8,000 and hold it until the GPS shows the shelf behind her.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Eight thousand is the floor. The floor's part of the building."</span>`,
          why:`The Class B begins at its floor. At 8,000 MSL under an 80 you're inside it. And level right at a floor is where the AIM warns you may meet aircraft operating at the Class B boundary. Leave a cushion below it.`},
        {t:`Level off where she is and ask Denver Approach for a Class Bravo clearance.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="woman">"Cessna Niner One Echo, remain outside Bravo airspace."</span> Which, sure. Somewhere over their heads an airliner is sliding down the shelf toward Denver with a couple hundred people in it. Abby: <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Asking is legal. Two hundred feet under the floor with your hands full is a bad place to wait for an answer."</span>`,
          why:`Asking for a clearance is a legitimate move, and stopping the climb was right. But hovering just under the floor leaves no margin, and the answer is often "remain outside." Get comfortably below first, then ask if you need it.`},
        {t:`Descend right away to 7,500 and stay well below the shelf floor.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`She shoves the nose over and pulls a little power, and the altimeter winds down through 7,700 and 7,600 and settles on 7,500, where it should have been all along. Her palms are wet on the yoke. She stops looking at the reservoir. Abby lifts her finger off the glass and puts it back on her own knee. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"What'd Approach say when you checked in?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Radar contact."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"And what's that mean?"</span> Jordan thinks about it longer than she'd like to. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"That they can see me."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Yep. They'd have watched the whole thing."</span> Out past the strut the wheat goes by in squares. After a while, in a voice Jordan hasn't heard out of her before, lower, almost kind: <span class="said" data-who="Abby" data-tone="Lower, quiet, almost kind.">"Two hundred feet. Everybody gets their two hundred feet once. That was yours."</span>`,
          why:`Stop the climb and get back under the floor with a margin. The floor over Centennial is 8,000 MSL, and the shelves step down as you get closer to Denver International, so check every floor on your route on a current chart. "Radar contact" is not a Class B clearance. Stay below the floor unless you hear the words "cleared to enter the Class Bravo airspace."`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills:[
    {after:`f4s12`, title:`How much room under the lid`,
     setup:[`After they landed, Abby made Jordan sit at the briefing room table and work the numbers on a clean sheet. Field elevation is 5,885 feet MSL. The Class B floor over Centennial is 8,000 MSL. Abby's rule is to stay at least 300 feet below any floor you aren't cleared into.`,`Biscuit climbs at about 500 feet per minute at full throttle on a summer morning, at about 75 knots groundspeed.`,`Then Abby wrote a morning METAR on the board: <code>METAR KAPA 181253Z 09005KT 10SM BKN030 16/08 A3020</code>`],
     fields:[{label:`Feet between the field and the Class B floor`,answer:2115,tol:0},{label:`Highest altitude under Abby's 300 foot rule (feet MSL)`,answer:7700,tol:0},{label:`Minutes to climb from the field to that altitude at 500 fpm`,answer:3.63,tol:0.01},{label:`Nautical miles covered in that climb at 75 knots`,answer:4.5,tol:0.05},{label:`Ceiling from the METAR, in feet MSL`,answer:8885,tol:0},{label:`Highest altitude that stays 500 feet below that layer (feet MSL)`,answer:8385,tol:0}],
     solution:[`Room under the lid: 8,000 minus 5,885 = 2,115 feet.`,`Abby's cap: 8,000 minus 300 = 7,700 feet MSL. That's 7,700 minus 5,885 = 1,815 feet of climb.`,`Time: 1,815 ÷ 500 = 3.63 minutes, a little over three and a half minutes.`,`Distance: 75 knots is 1.25 nautical miles per minute. 3.63 × 1.25 = 4.54, about 4.5 nautical miles. So you can be at the cap before you're five miles from the runway. That's the whole reason the shelf catches people.`,`Ceiling: BKN030 is 3,000 feet AGL. 3,000 + 5,885 = 8,885 feet MSL.`,`Cloud clearance: 500 feet below the clouds, so 8,885 minus 500 = 8,385 MSL. The clouds would allow 8,385, but the Class B floor at 8,000 comes first, and Abby's cap at 7,700 comes before that. The lowest limit always wins.`]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f4s2`, title:`Read the chart`, intro:`Abby took the sectional off the wall, laid it on the table, and pointed at one symbol at a time. Jordan had her own list from Tuesday in front of her and checked each answer against it.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`A blue fraction inside solid blue lines, with 80 on the bottom.`, choices:[`Class C, floor 8,000 feet AGL`,`Class B, floor 8,000 feet MSL`,`Class B, ceiling 8,000 feet MSL`], answer:1, why:`Class B is solid blue. The bottom number is the floor, the top number is the ceiling, both in hundreds of feet MSL.`},
      {prompt:`A dashed blue line around an airport, with a small box showing minus 80.`, choices:[`Class D up to but not including 8,000 MSL`,`Class D from 8,000 MSL down to the surface, including 8,000`,`Class E surface area, ceiling 8,000 AGL`], answer:0, why:`Class D is a dashed blue line. The boxed number is the ceiling in hundreds of feet MSL, and a minus sign means up to but not including that altitude.`},
      {prompt:`Solid magenta lines around an airport, with magenta fractions such as 70 over SFC.`, choices:[`Class E surface area`,`Class C airspace`,`A restricted area`], answer:1, why:`Class C is drawn in solid magenta, with ceilings and floors in hundreds of feet MSL. SFC means the floor is the surface.`},
      {prompt:`A dashed magenta line around an airport with no tower.`, choices:[`Class D airspace`,`Class E airspace beginning at 700 AGL`,`Class E airspace beginning at the surface`], answer:2, why:`A dashed magenta line marks a Class E surface area. Class D is dashed blue.`},
      {prompt:`A fuzzy magenta band, darker on one edge and fading out on the other.`, choices:[`Class E floor at 700 feet AGL`,`Class E floor at 1,200 feet AGL`,`The edge of a military operations area`], answer:0, why:`The magenta vignette marks where the Class E floor drops to 700 feet AGL. Class G is below it out to the edge.`},
      {prompt:`A fuzzy blue band where controlled airspace borders Class G.`, choices:[`Class B floor at 1,200 AGL`,`A Class E floor other than 700 AGL, usually 1,200`,`A Class E floor at the surface`], answer:1, why:`A blue vignette marks a Class E floor other than 700 feet that borders Class G. Usually that's 1,200 AGL.`},
      {prompt:`A thin solid magenta circle labeled MODE C & ADS-B OUT 30 NM.`, choices:[`Class C outer area`,`The limit of Denver Approach's radar coverage`,`Transponder and ADS-B Out required, surface to 10,000 MSL`], answer:2, why:`The Mode C veil: within 30 NM of an Appendix D airport, from the surface to 10,000 MSL, a transponder with altitude reporting and ADS-B Out are required.`},
      {prompt:`NO SVFR printed above an airport's name.`, choices:[`Fixed wing Special VFR is not permitted there`,`The airport has no VFR traffic pattern`,`Special VFR is available only at night`], answer:0, why:`The chart prints NO SVFR at airports listed in Appendix D, section 3, where fixed wing Special VFR isn't allowed. Denver International is one.`},
      {prompt:`An area labeled with an R and a number, like the PHAK's example, R-4401.`, choices:[`An alert area for student training`,`A restricted area with invisible hazards`,`A temporary flight restriction`], answer:1, why:`R followed by a number is a restricted area under Part 73. When it's active, you need permission from the using or controlling agency to enter.`},
      {prompt:`A large number in a quadrant, like 14 with a small 5 after it.`, choices:[`Class E floor of 14,500 MSL`,`A VFR cruising altitude of 14,500`,`Maximum elevation figure, 14,500 MSL`], answer:2, why:`The maximum elevation figure shows the highest terrain or obstacle in that quadrant, rounded up to the next 100 feet, with the last two digits left off. 14 and a small 5 is 14,500 MSL.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`What are the basic VFR weather minimums in Class B airspace?`, opts:[`3 SM visibility, 500 feet below, 1,000 above, 2,000 horizontal`,`1 SM visibility and clear of clouds`,`3 SM visibility and clear of clouds`], a:2, why:`91.155: Class B requires 3 statute miles flight visibility and clear of clouds. The 500, 1,000, and 2,000 foot clearances apply in Class C, D, and E below 10,000 MSL.`, task:`I.E`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`What minimum flight visibility and cloud clearance apply in Class D airspace?`, opts:[`3 SM; 500 below, 1,000 above, 2,000 horizontal`,`1 SM; clear of clouds`,`5 SM; 1,000 below, 1,000 above, 1 SM horizontal`], a:0, why:`Class C, Class D, and Class E below 10,000 MSL share the same minimums: 3 SM, 500 feet below, 1,000 feet above, and 2,000 feet horizontal from clouds.`, task:`I.E`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`During the day, in Class G airspace at 1,200 feet AGL or less, the VFR minimums for an airplane are`, opts:[`3 SM and clear of clouds`,`1 SM and clear of clouds`,`1 SM; 500 below, 1,000 above, 2,000 horizontal`], a:1, why:`In Class G at or below 1,200 AGL by day, an airplane needs 1 statute mile and clear of clouds. At night it's 3 SM and the 500, 1,000, and 2,000 foot clearances, except in a pattern within half a mile of the runway.`, task:`I.E`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`In Class E airspace at or above 10,000 feet MSL, VFR flight requires`, opts:[`5 SM; 1,000 below, 1,000 above, 1 SM horizontal`,`3 SM; 500 below, 1,000 above, 2,000 horizontal`,`5 SM; clear of clouds`], a:0, why:`At and above 10,000 MSL in Class E, 91.155 requires 5 statute miles and 1,000 feet below, 1,000 feet above, and 1 statute mile horizontal from clouds.`, task:`I.E`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Before entering Class B airspace, a VFR pilot must`, opts:[`establish two way radio contact with approach control`,`receive an ATC clearance to enter the Class B airspace`,`file a VFR flight plan and squawk 1200`], a:1, why:`91.131(a)(1) requires an ATC clearance before operating in Class B. Two way radio contact alone is the Class C and D standard, and "radar contact" is not a clearance.`, task:`I.E`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Approaching Class D airspace, a pilot calls the tower and hears "Aircraft calling Centennial Tower, standby." The pilot`, opts:[`may enter, because the tower has responded`,`may enter only after squawking 1200`,`must remain outside the Class D airspace`], a:2, why:`Two way communication is established only when the controller responds with your call sign. Without it you may not enter. With your call sign and "standby," you could.`, task:`III.A`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`The Mode C veil around a primary Class B airport extends`, opts:[`20 NM, from the surface to 10,000 feet MSL`,`30 NM, from the surface to 10,000 feet MSL`,`30 NM, from the Class B floor to its ceiling`], a:1, why:`91.215(b)(2) and 91.225(d)(2): within 30 NM of an Appendix D, section 1 airport, from the surface up to 10,000 feet MSL, a transponder with altitude reporting and ADS-B Out are required.`, task:`I.E`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`What is the maximum indicated airspeed in the airspace underlying a Class B airspace area?`, opts:[`200 knots`,`230 knots`,`250 knots`], a:0, why:`91.117(c) limits aircraft to 200 knots indicated in the airspace underlying Class B and in VFR corridors through it. 250 knots is the general limit below 10,000 MSL.`, task:`I.E`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`A student pilot may solo in a specific Class B airspace area only after receiving training and a logbook endorsement for it dated within the preceding`, opts:[`30 days`,`90 days`,`12 calendar months`], a:1, why:`61.95(a) requires ground and flight training on that specific Class B and an endorsement from the instructor who gave the training, dated within the 90 days before the flight.`, task:`I.A`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`While in flight, a pilot sees a flashing green light gun signal from the tower. This means`, opts:[`cleared to land`,`return for landing`,`give way and continue circling`], a:1, why:`In flight, flashing green means return for landing, followed by a steady green at the proper time. Steady green is cleared to land, and steady red is give way and continue circling.`, task:`III.A`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`A flashing red light from the tower directed at an aircraft in flight means`, opts:[`airport unsafe, do not land`,`give way to other aircraft and continue circling`,`exercise extreme caution`], a:0, why:`91.125: flashing red in flight means airport unsafe, do not land. Steady red means give way and continue circling. Alternating red and green means exercise extreme caution.`, task:`III.A`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`Under 91.157, a daytime Special VFR clearance in a surface area allows flight with`, opts:[`3 SM visibility and clear of clouds`,`1 SM visibility and 500 feet below clouds`,`1 SM, clear of clouds, with a clearance`], a:2, why:`Special VFR requires an ATC clearance, clear of clouds, and at least 1 statute mile flight visibility, within the surface area below 10,000 MSL. Takeoff and landing need 1 SM ground visibility.`, task:`I.E`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`A VFR pilot planning to fly through an active military operations area should`, opts:[`obtain a clearance from the using agency, as for a restricted area`,`exercise caution and contact the controlling agency for status`,`remain outside unless the MOA is listed as inactive by NOTAM`], a:1, why:`VFR flight in an MOA is permitted, but the AIM urges extreme caution when it's active and recommends contacting the controlling agency or an FSS within 100 miles for traffic and status.`, task:`I.E`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Temporary flight restrictions are disseminated as`, opts:[`pointer NOTAMs only`,`NOTAM (D) items for nearby airports`,`FDC NOTAMs`], a:2, why:`TFRs are issued as Flight Data Center NOTAMs, because they're regulatory. Pointer NOTAMs may point to them, and most TFRs are not charted, so checking NOTAMs is part of preflight.`, task:`I.E`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`A pilot receiving VFR flight following hears "radar contact" from approach control. This means`, opts:[`ATC sees the aircraft, without any clearance to enter Class B`,`the aircraft is cleared to climb into the overlying Class B`,`ATC will now provide separation from all other aircraft`], a:0, why:`Radar contact means the aircraft is identified on the controller's display. Traffic advisories are workload permitting, see and avoid still applies, and Class B entry still needs the words "cleared to enter" or "cleared through" the Bravo airspace.`, task:`VI`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`},
    {fig:`<prop type="doc" title="Figure: sectional chart excerpt, described">A magenta airport symbol for Millbrook (MBK), a small field with no tower. The airport sits inside a fuzzy magenta band, a magenta vignette. There's no dashed line around the airport.</prop>`, q:`Using the figure, where does Class E airspace begin over Millbrook Airport?`, opts:[`At the surface`,`1,200 feet AGL`,`700 feet AGL`], a:2, why:`A magenta vignette marks Class E airspace with a floor of 700 feet AGL, usually around an airport with an instrument approach. A dashed magenta line would mean Class E from the surface, and a blue vignette marks a floor other than 700, usually 1,200 AGL.`, task:`I.E`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {fig:`<prop type="doc" title="Figure: obstruction on a sectional chart, described">An obstruction symbol, an upside down V, beside a small town. Next to it, a bold 3,249 and, under it, (1,049) in parentheses.</prop>`, q:`Using the figure, how tall is the obstruction above the ground?`, opts:[`1,049 feet`,`3,249 feet`,`2,200 feet`], a:0, why:`Bold number on top: where the tower's top is, in feet MSL. Number in parentheses: how tall it is from the ground up. Subtract one from the other and you get 2,200, which is just the ground it stands on.`, task:`I.E`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`To be cleared for Special VFR at night, the pilot of an airplane must`, opts:[`hold a private certificate and have logged 50 hours at night`,`stay outside every Class D surface area for the whole flight`,`hold an instrument rating and fly an IFR equipped airplane`], a:2, why:`14 CFR 91.157(e): Special VFR between sunset and sunrise requires the pilot to be instrument rated and current and the airplane to be equipped for IFR. Special VFR is used inside surface areas, not outside them.`, task:`I.E`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`A military training route charted as VR1257 is flown`, opts:[`under VFR, with no segment above 1,500 feet AGL`,`under IFR, with at least one segment above 1,500 AGL`,`under VFR, with at least one segment above 1,500 AGL`], a:0, why:`AIM 3-5-2: VR routes are flown VFR and IR routes IFR. Four digits means no segment above 1,500 feet AGL; three digits means at least one segment is above 1,500 AGL.`, task:`I.E`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Participation in a terminal radar service area (TRSA) by a VFR pilot is`, opts:[`required whenever the TRSA radar is operating`,`voluntary, though pilots are urged to use it`,`required only between sunset and sunrise`], a:1, why:`AIM 3-5-6: VFR participation in a TRSA is voluntary, though pilots are urged to take part. A TRSA isn't one of the airspace classes; the airspace around its primary airport is Class D. TRSAs are charted with a black outline.`, task:`I.E`, domain:`Preflight`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `They flew the steep turns at 7,500 that day, under the shelf, which turned out to be fine, and Jordan's were the best she'd flown. Abby wrote it up in the briefing room, 1.5 dual, and in the remarks put CLASS B AWARENESS, which made it sound like a seminar.`,
    `Jordan said she'd be a minute. She walked back out to Biscuit, sat in the left seat with the door open, and took two things out of her flight bag. Danny's old sectional, folded to the Denver panel. And the green notebook.`,
    `She'd skipped ahead once before. Today she did it again. She went looking, the way she'd look for one line in a chart that she knew had to be there, and found it about a third of the way through.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="Danny's logbook, a page from August, eight years ago">August 9. KAPA local, 1.3. Gorgeous morning. Off 35R, right turn east. Approach gives me a squawk, I put it in, "radar contact," great, I'm in the system, I'm basically an airliner. Fiddling with the new GPS. Admiring the reservoir like a tourist. Approach comes back: "Cessna Niner One Echo, possible pilot deviation, advise you contact Denver TRACON at" and then a phone number, which I wrote on the back of my left hand with a grease pencil because I'm a professional. 8,300 feet. The floor over the field is 8,000. Called from the FBO, shaking. Guy was nice about it. Said "radar contact" means they can see you, and they sure did. Filed the NASA form that night. Walt helped and didn't say I told you so, which is how I knew it was bad. New rule: nothing above 7,500 until I'm past the shelf. Circled it on the chart so I can't miss it. Circled it again. And again.</prop>`,
    `Jordan read it twice. Then she unfolded his chart on her knees and looked at the red rings around the 80, three laps of them, and the pen going through the paper on the last one.`,
    `Off 35R, right turn east. Radar contact. The reservoir. She'd done it the same way. Not close to the same way. The same way, down to the thing she was looking at out the left window. He'd been a little over the line and she'd been a little under it, and the difference between those two mornings was a finger on the glass of the altimeter.`,
    (s.score>=27
      ?`She'd spent four months either keeping away from everything that looked like him or trying to fly it exactly the way he would have. It hadn't occurred to her that she could do both at once, without meaning to, on a clear Saturday at seven thousand eight hundred feet. She sat there with that for a while.`
      :`It had been an uneven few weeks, and she knew which calls she'd gotten wrong. She'd spent four months either keeping away from everything that looked like him or trying to fly it exactly the way he would have. It hadn't occurred to her that she could do both at once, without meaning to, at seven thousand eight hundred feet. She sat there with that for a while.`),
    `Then she got a pen out of the door pocket and wrote on her kneeboard, under the strip of blue tape from the static port, in her own small handwriting: <b>SHELF AT 80. SAY IT OUT LOUD BEFORE TAKEOFF. RADAR CONTACT ISN'T A YES.</b> It wasn't his rule. His rule was 7,500 and a red pen. Hers was saying it out loud, which was the thing she did at work before every drug she pushed. She thought he'd have made fun of her for it. She thought he'd have been right to, a little.`,
    `Abby came out a few minutes later with her car keys in her teeth and her flight bag over one shoulder, saw the notebook, and stopped by the strut.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Abby" data-tone="Quiet, fond, a little rueful.">"He told me about that one,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Abby" data-tone="Quiet, fond, a little rueful.">"The week my first student failed a checkride. He showed up with breakfast burritos and told me it wasn't my fault, and then, I guess so I'd feel better, he told me the whole thing. The grease pencil, the phone call. He said the only pilots who never got a phone number were the ones who didn't fly enough to get one."</span> She looked at the chart. <span class="said" data-who="Abby" data-tone="Quiet, fond, a little rueful.">"He didn't tell me about the red circles."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"He circled it three times."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Yeah."</span> Abby nodded at the kneeboard. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"I like yours better. Same lesson. You can say yours out loud."</span> She rapped the cowling twice with her knuckles on the way past, like it was a horse that had done fine, and went to find her car.`,
    `At 9:14 that night the phone went off on the kitchen table. The sectional was next to it. She'd gotten it folded back up right on the fourth try and was unreasonably proud of that.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Abby Strand" time="9:14 PM">Next is performance. August, 95 degrees, 5,885 feet. Biscuit hates all three. Bring a calculator and whatever questions Ellie's been saving up.</prop>`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch04.mp3`, len:`about 50 minutes`},
  next:`Performance`
});
